derate-to-severe disease. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male is brought to the clinic by a family member due to progressive memory loss over the past 2 years. The family reports that the patient frequently misplaces items, repeats questions, and has difficulty managing finances. Cognitive testing reveals a MoCA score of **15/30**. A brain MRI shows bilateral **hippocampal and temporoparietal** atrophy. Which of the following describes the characteristic histopathological findings and first-line pharmacological treatment for this condition?', '["Perivascular lymphocytic cuffs and demyelinating plaques; treat with interferon-beta", "Ubiquitin and tau-positive inclusions in the frontotemporal lobes; treat with high-dose atypical antipsychotics", "Alpha-synuclein cytoplasmic inclusions (Lewy bodies) in the substantia nigra; treat with high-dose levodopa/carbidopa", "Extracellular amyloid-beta plaques and intracellular hyperphosphorylated tau neurofibrillary tangles; treat with cholinesterase inhibitors (e.g., Donepezil)", "Spongiform encephalopathy with vacuolation of neurons; treat with acyclovir and corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient''''s clinical presentation—insidious onset, slowly progressive memory impairment, and bilateral **hippocampal and temporoparietal** atrophy on brain MRI—is diagnostic of **Alzheimer''''s disease (AD)**, the most common cause of dementia in older adults (60-80% of cases). The hallmark histopathological features include: (1) **Extracellular amyloid-beta (Aβ) plaques** derived from abnormal cleavage of amyloid precursor protein (APP) by β-secretase and γ-secretase, and (2) **Intracellular neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs)** consisting of **hyperphosphorylated tau protein**. These accumulate preferentially in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus early in the disease. First-line pharmacological management for mild-to-moderate AD includes **cholinesterase inhibitors** (Donepezil, Galantamine, or Rivastigmine). Memantine (NMDA antagonist) is added for moderate-to-severe disease. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old man presents with a 3-year history of progressive memory loss and word-finding difficulty. Cognitive testing shows impairment in delayed recall and executive function. MRI shows mild hippocampal atrophy. To support the clinical diagnosis and identify underlying Alzheimer''''s pathology, a lumbar puncture is performed. Which of the following CSF biomarker profiles is most consistent with Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Increased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), decreased total tau, and decreased phosphorylated tau (p-tau)", "Normal CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), markedly elevated 14-3-3 protein, and normal phosphorylated tau", "Elevated CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), normal total tau, and elevated oligoclonal bands", "Decreased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), increased total tau, and increased phosphorylated tau (p-tau)", "Decreased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), decreased total tau, and elevated CSF glucose"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The characteristic CSF biomarker profile for **Alzheimer''''s disease** is the **ATN framework**: (A) **Decreased CSF Aβ42** — amyloid-beta 42 is sequestered into brain plaques, reducing its CSF levels; (T) **Increased phosphorylated tau (p-tau 181 or p-tau 217)** — reflects tau tangle pathology; (N) **Increased total tau (t-tau)** — reflects neurodegeneration. This profile has high sensitivity (~95%) and specificity (~85%) for AD pathology. **Amyloid PET** (using florbetapir or flutemetamol) can also confirm cerebral amyloid deposition. Elevated 14-3-3 protein is characteristic of CJD, not AD. Oligoclonal bands suggest multiple sclerosis. These biomarkers are particularly useful in atypical presentations or when diagnostic certainty is needed for anti-amyloid therapy eligibility. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; NIA-AA Research Framework 2018)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old female presents with a 3-year history of progressive memory loss and word-finding difficulty. Cognitive testing shows impairment in delayed recall and executive function. MRI shows mild hippocampal atrophy. To support the clinical diagnosis and identify underlying Alzheimer''''s pathology, a lumbar puncture is performed. Which of the following CSF biomarker profiles is most consistent with Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Increased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), decreased total tau, and decreased phosphorylated tau (p-tau)", "Decreased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), decreased total tau, and elevated CSF glucose", "Decreased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), increased total tau, and increased phosphorylated tau (p-tau)", "Normal CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), markedly elevated 14-3-3 protein, and normal phosphorylated tau", "Elevated CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), normal total tau, and elevated oligoclonal bands"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The characteristic CSF biomarker profile for **Alzheimer''''s disease** is the **ATN framework**: (A) **Decreased CSF Aβ42** — amyloid-beta 42 is sequestered into brain plaques, reducing its CSF levels; (T) **Increased phosphorylated tau (p-tau 181 or p-tau 217)** — reflects tau tangle pathology; (N) **Increased total tau (t-tau)** — reflects neurodegeneration. This profile has high sensitivity (~95%) and specificity (~85%) for AD pathology. **Amyloid PET** (using florbetapir or flutemetamol) can also confirm cerebral amyloid deposition. Elevated 14-3-3 protein is characteristic of CJD, not AD. Oligoclonal bands suggest multiple sclerosis. These biomarkers are particularly useful in atypical presentations or when diagnostic certainty is needed for anti-amyloid therapy eligibility. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; NIA-AA Research Framework 2018)'),
