    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old woman presents with a 3-year history of progressive memory loss and word-finding difficulty. Cognitive testing shows impairment in delayed recall and executive function. MRI shows mild hippocampal atrophy. To support the clinical diagnosis and identify underlying Alzheimer''''s pathology, a lumbar puncture is performed. Which of the following CSF biomarker profiles is most consistent with Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Elevated CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), normal total tau, and elevated oligoclonal bands", "Decreased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), decreased total tau, and elevated CSF glucose", "Increased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), decreased total tau, and decreased phosphorylated tau (p-tau)", "Decreased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), increased total tau, and increased phosphorylated tau (p-tau)", "Normal CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), markedly elevated 14-3-3 protein, and normal phosphorylated tau"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The characteristic CSF biomarker profile for **Alzheimer''''s disease** is the **ATN framework**: (A) **Decreased CSF Aβ42** — amyloid-beta 42 is sequestered into brain plaques, reducing its CSF levels; (T) **Increased phosphorylated tau (p-tau 181 or p-tau 217)** — reflects tau tangle pathology; (N) **Increased total tau (t-tau)** — reflects neurodegeneration. This profile has high sensitivity (~95%) and specificity (~85%) for AD pathology. **Amyloid PET** (using florbetapir or flutemetamol) can also confirm cerebral amyloid deposition. Elevated 14-3-3 protein is characteristic of CJD, not AD. Oligoclonal bands suggest multiple sclerosis. These biomarkers are particularly useful in atypical presentations or when diagnostic certainty is needed for anti-amyloid therapy eligibility. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; NIA-AA Research Framework 2018)'),
