    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old man presents with a 3-year history of progressive memory loss and word-finding difficulty. Cognitive testing shows impairment in delayed recall and executive function. MRI shows mild hippocampal atrophy. To support the clinical diagnosis and identify underlying Alzheimer''''s pathology, a lumbar puncture is performed. Which of the following CSF biomarker profiles is most consistent with Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Decreased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), increased total tau, and increased phosphorylated tau (p-tau)", "Normal CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), markedly elevated 14-3-3 protein, and normal phosphorylated tau", "Decreased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), decreased total tau, and elevated CSF glucose", "Increased CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), decreased total tau, and decreased phosphorylated tau (p-tau)", "Elevated CSF amyloid-beta 42 (A\u03b242), normal total tau, and elevated oligoclonal bands"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The characteristic CSF biomarker profile for **Alzheimer''''s disease** is the **ATN framework**: (A) **Decreased CSF Aβ42** — amyloid-beta 42 is sequestered into brain plaques, reducing its CSF levels; (T) **Increased phosphorylated tau (p-tau 181 or p-tau 217)** — reflects tau tangle pathology; (N) **Increased total tau (t-tau)** — reflects neurodegeneration. This profile has high sensitivity (~95%) and specificity (~85%) for AD pathology. **Amyloid PET** (using florbetapir or flutemetamol) can also confirm cerebral amyloid deposition. Elevated 14-3-3 protein is characteristic of CJD, not AD. Oligoclonal bands suggest multiple sclerosis. These biomarkers are particularly useful in atypical presentations or when diagnostic certainty is needed for anti-amyloid therapy eligibility. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; NIA-AA Research Framework 2018)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old man presents with progressive memory loss, visuospatial disorientation, and difficulty with multistep tasks over the past 3 years. Family history reveals that the patient''''s mother developed dementia at age 52 and a sibling was diagnosed at age 48. Genetic testing is performed. Which of the following statements regarding the genetics of Alzheimer''''s disease is most accurate?', '["Late-onset AD is caused exclusively by autosomal dominant mutations in the APOE gene on chromosome 19, with 100% penetrance in homozygous \u03b54 carriers", "Patients with Down syndrome (trisomy 21) have a decreased risk of AD due to overexpression of protective superoxide dismutase genes", "The APOE \u03b52 allele is the strongest risk factor for both early-onset and late-onset AD and is associated with earlier age of onset", "Early-onset familial AD (<65 years) is most commonly caused by autosomal dominant mutations in presenilin-1 (PSEN1) on chromosome 14, presenilin-2 (PSEN2) on chromosome 1, or amyloid precursor protein (APP) on chromosome 21; the APOE \u03b54 allele is the strongest genetic risk factor for late-onset sporadic AD", "Early-onset AD is caused by trinucleotide repeat expansions in the C9orf72 gene, which is also linked to amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Early-onset familial Alzheimer''''s disease** (<65 years, ~5% of AD cases) follows **autosomal dominant** inheritance and is most commonly caused by mutations in: (1) **PSEN1** (presenilin-1, chromosome 14) — the MOST common cause of early-onset familial AD, (2) **APP** (amyloid precursor protein, chromosome 21), or (3) **PSEN2** (presenilin-2, chromosome 1). These mutations increase production of the toxic Aβ42 fragment. **Late-onset sporadic AD** (>65 years, ~95% of cases) is influenced by the **APOE ε4 allele** (chromosome 19) — the strongest **genetic risk factor** (not deterministic). Each ε4 copy increases risk ~3-4x; homozygosity (ε4/ε4) increases risk ~12-15x. The **APOE ε2 allele is PROTECTIVE**. Patients with **Down syndrome (trisomy 21)** have an extra copy of APP, leading to increased Aβ production and nearly universal AD pathology by age 40. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
