    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female presents with progressive memory loss, visuospatial disorientation, and difficulty with multistep tasks over the past 4 years. Family history reveals that the patient''''s mother developed dementia at age 52 and a sibling was diagnosed at age 48. Genetic testing is performed. Which of the following statements regarding the genetics of Alzheimer''''s disease is most accurate?', '["Patients with Down syndrome (trisomy 21) have a decreased risk of AD due to overexpression of protective superoxide dismutase genes", "The APOE \u03b52 allele is the strongest risk factor for both early-onset and late-onset AD and is associated with earlier age of onset", "Early-onset familial AD (<65 years) is most commonly caused by autosomal dominant mutations in presenilin-1 (PSEN1) on chromosome 14, presenilin-2 (PSEN2) on chromosome 1, or amyloid precursor protein (APP) on chromosome 21; the APOE \u03b54 allele is the strongest genetic risk factor for late-onset sporadic AD", "Early-onset AD is caused by trinucleotide repeat expansions in the C9orf72 gene, which is also linked to amyotrophic lateral sclerosis", "Late-onset AD is caused exclusively by autosomal dominant mutations in the APOE gene on chromosome 19, with 100% penetrance in homozygous \u03b54 carriers"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Early-onset familial Alzheimer''''s disease** (<65 years, ~5% of AD cases) follows **autosomal dominant** inheritance and is most commonly caused by mutations in: (1) **PSEN1** (presenilin-1, chromosome 14) — the MOST common cause of early-onset familial AD, (2) **APP** (amyloid precursor protein, chromosome 21), or (3) **PSEN2** (presenilin-2, chromosome 1). These mutations increase production of the toxic Aβ42 fragment. **Late-onset sporadic AD** (>65 years, ~95% of cases) is influenced by the **APOE ε4 allele** (chromosome 19) — the strongest **genetic risk factor** (not deterministic). Each ε4 copy increases risk ~3-4x; homozygosity (ε4/ε4) increases risk ~12-15x. The **APOE ε2 allele is PROTECTIVE**. Patients with **Down syndrome (trisomy 21)** have an extra copy of APP, leading to increased Aβ production and nearly universal AD pathology by age 40. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old man with Alzheimer''''s disease is evaluated for disease progression. The caregiver reports that the patient now needs assistance with dressing (choosing appropriate clothing), frequently gets lost in familiar neighborhoods, cannot manage finances, but can still eat independently, walk without assistance, and maintain basic hygiene with reminders. Which stage of Alzheimer''''s disease does this presentation represent, and what is the appropriate pharmacological approach?', '["Mild Cognitive Impairment; no pharmacotherapy is indicated; manage with lifestyle modifications alone", "Severe-stage AD; discontinue cholinesterase inhibitors and transition to comfort-focused palliative care only", "Moderate-stage AD; discontinue cholinesterase inhibitors and replace with high-dose atypical antipsychotics for behavioral management", "Moderate-stage AD; add memantine to the existing cholinesterase inhibitor regimen (combination therapy with donepezil + memantine)", "Mild-stage AD; continue cholinesterase inhibitor monotherapy and defer memantine until the patient becomes non-ambulatory"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient is in **moderate-stage Alzheimer''''s disease**. AD staging is defined functionally: **Mild AD** — difficulty with complex IADLs (finances, medications, driving) but basic ADLs preserved and largely independent; **Moderate AD** — needs help with some basic ADLs (dressing, bathing), gets lost, impaired judgment, but can eat and ambulate; **Severe AD** — dependent for all ADLs, minimal verbal output, incontinent, bedbound. For moderate-to-severe AD, guidelines recommend **adding memantine** (NMDA receptor antagonist) to the existing cholinesterase inhibitor. The **donepezil + memantine combination** has shown modest but statistically significant benefits in cognition and global function compared to cholinesterase inhibitor alone. Antipsychotics should NOT be used as primary cognitive therapy. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
