ster the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old man with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''''s disease lives at home with his spouse. The spouse asks about non-pharmacological strategies to improve the patient''''s quality of life, maintain function, and reduce behavioral symptoms. Which of the following represents the most evidence-based approach?', '["Structured daily routines, cognitive stimulation therapy (group activities, reminiscence), regular physical exercise, caregiver education, and environmental modifications (clear signage, nightlights, door alarms for wandering)", "High-dose melatonin (20 mg) and diphenhydramine (50 mg) nightly to ensure 12 hours of sleep and reduce daytime behavioral symptoms", "Intensive daily cognitive rehabilitation drills (e.g., 4 hours of memory flashcards) to reverse neurodegeneration and restore hippocampal volume", "Immediate placement in a skilled nursing facility regardless of disease stage to ensure professional supervision at all times", "Strict social isolation to reduce overstimulation, elimination of all physical activity to prevent falls, and locked room confinement during nighttime hours"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Non-pharmacological interventions** are the cornerstone of dementia care and should be implemented for ALL patients. Evidence-based strategies include: (1) **Structured daily routines** — predictability reduces anxiety and agitation; (2) **Cognitive stimulation therapy** — group-based activities including reminiscence, word games, and discussion have the strongest evidence for maintaining cognition; (3) **Regular physical exercise** — aerobic activity (walking, swimming) improves mood, reduces agitation, and may slow functional decline; (4) **Caregiver education** — training in communication techniques, behavioral management, and stress reduction; (5) **Environmental modifications** — clear signage, nightlights, door alarms, removing clutter to prevent falls and wandering; (6) **Music therapy** — reduces agitation and improves mood even in advanced disease. Social isolation, excessive sedation (especially with anticholinergics like diphenhydramine per Beers Criteria), and premature institutionalization are harmful. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''''s Ch. 73)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old man with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''''s disease lives at home with his spouse. The spouse asks about non-pharmacological strategies to improve the patient''''s quality of life, maintain function, and reduce behavioral symptoms. Which of the following represents the most evidence-based approach?', '["Immediate placement in a skilled nursing facility regardless of disease stage to ensure professional supervision at all times", "High-dose melatonin (20 mg) and diphenhydramine (50 mg) nightly to ensure 12 hours of sleep and reduce daytime behavioral symptoms", "Strict social isolation to reduce overstimulation, elimination of all physical activity to prevent falls, and locked room confinement during nighttime hours", "Intensive daily cognitive rehabilitation drills (e.g., 4 hours of memory flashcards) to reverse neurodegeneration and restore hippocampal volume", "Structured daily routines, cognitive stimulation therapy (group activities, reminiscence), regular physical exercise, caregiver education, and environmental modifications (clear signage, nightlights, door alarms for wandering)"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Non-pharmacological interventions** are the cornerstone of dementia care and should be implemented for ALL patients. Evidence-based strategies include: (1) **Structured daily routines** — predictability reduces anxiety and agitation; (2) **Cognitive stimulation therapy** — group-based activities including reminiscence, word games, and discussion have the strongest evidence for maintaining cognition; (3) **Regular physical exercise** — aerobic activity (walking, swimming) improves mood, reduces agitation, and may slow functional decline; (4) **Caregiver education** — training in communication techniques, behavioral management, and stress reduction; (5) **Environmental modifications** — clear signage, nightlights, door alarms, removing clutter to prevent falls and wandering; (6) **Music therapy** — reduces agitation and improves mood even in advanced disease. Social isolation, excessive sedation (especially with anticholinergics like diphenhydramine per Beers Criteria), and premature institutionalization are harmful. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''''s Ch. 73)'),
