    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old woman with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer''''s disease lives at home with her spouse. The spouse asks about non-pharmacological strategies to improve the patient''''s quality of life, maintain function, and reduce behavioral symptoms. Which of the following represents the most evidence-based approach?', '["Strict social isolation to reduce overstimulation, elimination of all physical activity to prevent falls, and locked room confinement during nighttime hours", "Structured daily routines, cognitive stimulation therapy (group activities, reminiscence), regular physical exercise, caregiver education, and environmental modifications (clear signage, nightlights, door alarms for wandering)", "Immediate placement in a skilled nursing facility regardless of disease stage to ensure professional supervision at all times", "Intensive daily cognitive rehabilitation drills (e.g., 4 hours of memory flashcards) to reverse neurodegeneration and restore hippocampal volume", "High-dose melatonin (20 mg) and diphenhydramine (50 mg) nightly to ensure 12 hours of sleep and reduce daytime behavioral symptoms"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Non-pharmacological interventions** are the cornerstone of dementia care and should be implemented for ALL patients. Evidence-based strategies include: (1) **Structured daily routines** — predictability reduces anxiety and agitation; (2) **Cognitive stimulation therapy** — group-based activities including reminiscence, word games, and discussion have the strongest evidence for maintaining cognition; (3) **Regular physical exercise** — aerobic activity (walking, swimming) improves mood, reduces agitation, and may slow functional decline; (4) **Caregiver education** — training in communication techniques, behavioral management, and stress reduction; (5) **Environmental modifications** — clear signage, nightlights, door alarms, removing clutter to prevent falls and wandering; (6) **Music therapy** — reduces agitation and improves mood even in advanced disease. Social isolation, excessive sedation (especially with anticholinergics like diphenhydramine per Beers Criteria), and premature institutionalization are harmful. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''''s Ch. 73)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old woman with moderate Alzheimer''''s disease has developed increasing agitation, pacing, and verbal aggression, particularly in the late afternoon and evening (**sundowning**). The caregiver has tried structured routines and redirection with limited success. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Initiate high-dose haloperidol 10 mg daily as first-line treatment for sundowning behavior in all dementia patients", "Prescribe diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly to sedate the patient and eliminate sundowning episodes entirely", "Trial of a low-dose SSRI (e.g., citalopram or sertraline) for agitation, while continuing non-pharmacological strategies; if psychotic features (delusions, threatening hallucinations) are present, consider low-dose quetiapine with informed consent about the FDA black box warning for increased mortality risk", "Start lorazepam 2 mg three times daily for anxiety-related agitation, as benzodiazepines are the safest anxiolytic class in elderly patients with dementia", "Immediately initiate physical restraints during evening hours to prevent wandering and aggression"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Behavioral and Psychological Symptoms of Dementia (BPSD)** — including agitation, aggression, psychosis, and **sundowning** (worsening confusion/agitation in late afternoon/evening) — affect up to 90% of dementia patients. Management follows a stepwise approach: (1) **First-line: non-pharmacological** — structured routines, adequate lighting, reducing stimulation, music therapy, redirection; (2) **Second-line: pharmacotherapy** — **SSRIs** (citalopram ≤20mg in elderly, sertraline) have evidence for reducing agitation. **Antipsychotics** (quetiapine, risperidone) may be used for psychotic features (delusions, hallucinations) but carry an **FDA black box warning** for increased risk of cerebrovascular events and death in elderly patients with dementia. Must obtain informed consent. **AVOID per Beers Criteria**: benzodiazepines (paradoxical agitation, falls, respiratory depression), anticholinergics like diphenhydramine (worsen cognition, cause delirium), and physical restraints (increase agitation, injury, death). (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''''s Ch. 73)'),
