    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old male with moderate Alzheimer''''s disease has developed increasing agitation, pacing, and verbal aggression, particularly in the late afternoon and evening (**sundowning**). The caregiver has tried structured routines and redirection with limited success. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Immediately initiate physical restraints during evening hours to prevent wandering and aggression", "Start lorazepam 2 mg three times daily for anxiety-related agitation, as benzodiazepines are the safest anxiolytic class in elderly patients with dementia", "Initiate high-dose haloperidol 10 mg daily as first-line treatment for sundowning behavior in all dementia patients", "Trial of a low-dose SSRI (e.g., citalopram or sertraline) for agitation, while continuing non-pharmacological strategies; if psychotic features (delusions, threatening hallucinations) are present, consider low-dose quetiapine with informed consent about the FDA black box warning for increased mortality risk", "Prescribe diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly to sedate the patient and eliminate sundowning episodes entirely"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Behavioral and Psychological Symptoms of Dementia (BPSD)** — including agitation, aggression, psychosis, and **sundowning** (worsening confusion/agitation in late afternoon/evening) — affect up to 90% of dementia patients. Management follows a stepwise approach: (1) **First-line: non-pharmacological** — structured routines, adequate lighting, reducing stimulation, music therapy, redirection; (2) **Second-line: pharmacotherapy** — **SSRIs** (citalopram ≤20mg in elderly, sertraline) have evidence for reducing agitation. **Antipsychotics** (quetiapine, risperidone) may be used for psychotic features (delusions, hallucinations) but carry an **FDA black box warning** for increased risk of cerebrovascular events and death in elderly patients with dementia. Must obtain informed consent. **AVOID per Beers Criteria**: benzodiazepines (paradoxical agitation, falls, respiratory depression), anticholinergics like diphenhydramine (worsen cognition, cause delirium), and physical restraints (increase agitation, injury, death). (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''''s Ch. 73)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old man with moderate Alzheimer''''s disease is accompanied to the clinic by his primary caregiver (spouse). During the visit, the caregiver appears exhausted, tearful, and reports feeling overwhelmed, sleeping poorly, and having lost 15 pounds over the past 3 months. The caregiver says, ''''I feel like I have no life anymore.'''' Which of the following is the most important intervention at this visit?', '["Focus exclusively on the patient''''s cognitive testing and medication adjustments; the caregiver''''s wellbeing is outside the scope of this visit", "Advise the caregiver to hire full-time private nursing staff immediately, regardless of financial circumstances", "Reassure the caregiver that these feelings are normal and will resolve on their own once the patient is placed in a nursing home", "Recommend that the caregiver increase the patient''''s donepezil dose to reduce behavioral symptoms, which will indirectly relieve caregiver burden", "Screen the caregiver for depression and caregiver burnout; refer to caregiver support groups, arrange respite care (Adult Day Programs or in-home aides), and consider antidepressant therapy for the caregiver if depression is confirmed"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Caregiver burnout** is extremely common in dementia care and is a medical emergency in its own right. Over 40% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression, and caregiver stress is independently associated with increased mortality. At every dementia visit, clinicians should: (1) **Screen the caregiver** for depression (PHQ-9), anxiety, and burnout; (2) **Refer to support groups** (e.g., Alzheimer''''s Association, local support networks); (3) **Arrange respite care** — Adult Day Programs, in-home aides, or short-term residential respite to give the caregiver breaks; (4) **Treat caregiver depression** — SSRIs, counseling, and self-care strategies; (5) **Provide education** about disease progression, communication techniques, and advance care planning. Ignoring caregiver wellbeing leads to premature institutionalization of the patient and adverse health outcomes for both. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''''s Ch. 73; Alzheimer''''s Association Guidelines)'),
