    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old man with moderate Alzheimer''''s disease is accompanied to the clinic by his primary caregiver (spouse). During the visit, the caregiver appears exhausted, tearful, and reports feeling overwhelmed, sleeping poorly, and having lost 15 pounds over the past 3 months. The caregiver says, ''''I feel like I have no life anymore.'''' Which of the following is the most important intervention at this visit?', '["Advise the caregiver to hire full-time private nursing staff immediately, regardless of financial circumstances", "Screen the caregiver for depression and caregiver burnout; refer to caregiver support groups, arrange respite care (Adult Day Programs or in-home aides), and consider antidepressant therapy for the caregiver if depression is confirmed", "Recommend that the caregiver increase the patient''''s donepezil dose to reduce behavioral symptoms, which will indirectly relieve caregiver burden", "Focus exclusively on the patient''''s cognitive testing and medication adjustments; the caregiver''''s wellbeing is outside the scope of this visit", "Reassure the caregiver that these feelings are normal and will resolve on their own once the patient is placed in a nursing home"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Caregiver burnout** is extremely common in dementia care and is a medical emergency in its own right. Over 40% of dementia caregivers develop clinical depression, and caregiver stress is independently associated with increased mortality. At every dementia visit, clinicians should: (1) **Screen the caregiver** for depression (PHQ-9), anxiety, and burnout; (2) **Refer to support groups** (e.g., Alzheimer''''s Association, local support networks); (3) **Arrange respite care** — Adult Day Programs, in-home aides, or short-term residential respite to give the caregiver breaks; (4) **Treat caregiver depression** — SSRIs, counseling, and self-care strategies; (5) **Provide education** about disease progression, communication techniques, and advance care planning. Ignoring caregiver wellbeing leads to premature institutionalization of the patient and adverse health outcomes for both. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''''s Ch. 73; Alzheimer''''s Association Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old male with no significant past medical history presents with a 3-year history of progressive memory loss, word-finding difficulty, and visuospatial disorientation. Family history is remarkable for dementia in the patient''''s mother (onset at age 50) and maternal uncle (onset at age 53). Brain MRI shows prominent parietal and posterior cortical atrophy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding the genetic evaluation of this patient?', '["Genetic counseling and testing for autosomal dominant mutations in PSEN1 (most common cause of early-onset familial AD), PSEN2, and APP, given the strong family history and young age of onset", "Test for C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion, as this is the most common cause of all forms of Alzheimer''''s disease", "Karyotyping to rule out Down syndrome (trisomy 21) in all patients presenting with early-onset dementia", "APOE genotyping alone is sufficient for diagnosis, as the APOE \u03b54 allele is deterministic for early-onset AD", "No genetic testing is indicated because Alzheimer''''s disease is never hereditary and all cases are sporadic"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Early-onset Alzheimer''''s disease (EOAD)** is defined as AD with symptom onset **before age 65** and accounts for ~5-10% of all AD cases. When there is a strong autosomal dominant family history (multiple affected members across generations with onset <65), genetic testing should be pursued after appropriate **genetic counseling**. The most common mutations are: (1) **PSEN1** (presenilin-1, chromosome 14) — accounts for ~80% of early-onset familial AD cases, with nearly 100% penetrance; (2) **APP** (chromosome 21) — ~10-15%; (3) **PSEN2** (chromosome 1) — rarest, variable penetrance. APOE ε4 is a **risk factor** for late-onset sporadic AD but is NOT deterministic and NOT routinely used for diagnosis. C9orf72 is associated with FTD and ALS, not AD. Posterior cortical atrophy (PCA) is a recognized variant of early-onset AD presenting with prominent visuospatial deficits. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 58-year-old male with no significant past medical history presents with a 3-year history of progressive memory loss, word-finding difficulty, and visuospatial disorientation. Family history is remarkable for dementia in the patient''''s mother (onset at age 50) and maternal uncle (onset at age 53). Brain MRI shows prominent parietal and posterior cortical atrophy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding the genetic evaluation of this patient?', '["Genetic counseling and testing for autosomal dominant mutations in PSEN1 (most common cause of early-onset familial AD), PSEN2, and APP, given the strong family history and young age of onset", "APOE genotyping alone is sufficient for diagnosis, as the APOE \u03b54 allele is deterministic for early-onset AD", "Test for C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion, as this is the most common cause of all forms of Alzheimer''''s disease", "No genetic testing is indicated because Alzheimer''''s disease is never hereditary and all cases are sporadic", "Karyotyping to rule out Down syndrome (trisomy 21) in all patients presenting with early-onset dementia"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Early-onset Alzheimer''''s disease (EOAD)** is defined as AD with symptom onset **before age 65** and accounts for ~5-10% of all AD cases. When there is a strong autosomal dominant family history (multiple affected members across generations with onset <65), genetic testing should be pursued after appropriate **genetic counseling**. The most common mutations are: (1) **PSEN1** (presenilin-1, chromosome 14) — accounts for ~80% of early-onset familial AD cases, with nearly 100% penetrance; (2) **APP** (chromosome 21) — ~10-15%; (3) **PSEN2** (chromosome 1) — rarest, variable penetrance. APOE ε4 is a **risk factor** for late-onset sporadic AD but is NOT deterministic and NOT routinely used for diagnosis. C9orf72 is associated with FTD and ALS, not AD. Posterior cortical atrophy (PCA) is a recognized variant of early-onset AD presenting with prominent visuospatial deficits. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 51-year-old man with no significant past medical history presents with a 2-year history of progressive memory loss, word-finding difficulty, and visuospatial disorientation. Family history is remarkable for dementia in the patient''''s mother (onset at age 50) and maternal uncle (onset at age 53). Brain MRI shows prominent parietal and posterior cortical atrophy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding the genetic evaluation of this patient?', '["Karyotyping to rule out Down syndrome (trisomy 21) in all patients presenting with early-onset dementia", "Genetic counseling and testing for autosomal dominant mutations in PSEN1 (most common cause of early-onset familial AD), PSEN2, and APP, given the strong family history and young age of onset", "Test for C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion, as this is the most common cause of all forms of Alzheimer''''s disease", "No genetic testing is indicated because Alzheimer''''s disease is never hereditary and all cases are sporadic", "APOE genotyping alone is sufficient for diagnosis, as the APOE \u03b54 allele is deterministic for early-onset AD"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Early-onset Alzheimer''''s disease (EOAD)** is defined as AD with symptom onset **before age 65** and accounts for ~5-10% of all AD cases. When there is a strong autosomal dominant family history (multiple affected members across generations with onset <65), genetic testing should be pursued after appropriate **genetic counseling**. The most common mutations are: (1) **PSEN1** (presenilin-1, chromosome 14) — accounts for ~80% of early-onset familial AD cases, with nearly 100% penetrance; (2) **APP** (chromosome 21) — ~10-15%; (3) **PSEN2** (chromosome 1) — rarest, variable penetrance. APOE ε4 is a **risk factor** for late-onset sporadic AD but is NOT deterministic and NOT routinely used for diagnosis. C9orf72 is associated with FTD and ALS, not AD. Posterior cortical atrophy (PCA) is a recognized variant of early-onset 