    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old man with no significant past medical history presents with a 4-year history of progressive memory loss, word-finding difficulty, and visuospatial disorientation. Family history is remarkable for dementia in the patient''''s mother (onset at age 50) and maternal uncle (onset at age 53). Brain MRI shows prominent parietal and posterior cortical atrophy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding the genetic evaluation of this patient?', '["Test for C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion, as this is the most common cause of all forms of Alzheimer''''s disease", "Genetic counseling and testing for autosomal dominant mutations in PSEN1 (most common cause of early-onset familial AD), PSEN2, and APP, given the strong family history and young age of onset", "Karyotyping to rule out Down syndrome (trisomy 21) in all patients presenting with early-onset dementia", "APOE genotyping alone is sufficient for diagnosis, as the APOE \u03b54 allele is deterministic for early-onset AD", "No genetic testing is indicated because Alzheimer''''s disease is never hereditary and all cases are sporadic"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Early-onset Alzheimer''''s disease (EOAD)** is defined as AD with symptom onset **before age 65** and accounts for ~5-10% of all AD cases. When there is a strong autosomal dominant family history (multiple affected members across generations with onset <65), genetic testing should be pursued after appropriate **genetic counseling**. The most common mutations are: (1) **PSEN1** (presenilin-1, chromosome 14) — accounts for ~80% of early-onset familial AD cases, with nearly 100% penetrance; (2) **APP** (chromosome 21) — ~10-15%; (3) **PSEN2** (chromosome 1) — rarest, variable penetrance. APOE ε4 is a **risk factor** for late-onset sporadic AD but is NOT deterministic and NOT routinely used for diagnosis. C9orf72 is associated with FTD and ALS, not AD. Posterior cortical atrophy (PCA) is a recognized variant of early-onset AD presenting with prominent visuospatial deficits. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
