et AD presenting with prominent visuospatial deficits. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old male with no significant past medical history presents with a 2-year history of progressive memory loss, word-finding difficulty, and visuospatial disorientation. Family history is remarkable for dementia in the patient''''s mother (onset at age 50) and maternal uncle (onset at age 53). Brain MRI shows prominent parietal and posterior cortical atrophy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding the genetic evaluation of this patient?', '["APOE genotyping alone is sufficient for diagnosis, as the APOE \u03b54 allele is deterministic for early-onset AD", "Karyotyping to rule out Down syndrome (trisomy 21) in all patients presenting with early-onset dementia", "Test for C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion, as this is the most common cause of all forms of Alzheimer''''s disease", "Genetic counseling and testing for autosomal dominant mutations in PSEN1 (most common cause of early-onset familial AD), PSEN2, and APP, given the strong family history and young age of onset", "No genetic testing is indicated because Alzheimer''''s disease is never hereditary and all cases are sporadic"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Early-onset Alzheimer''''s disease (EOAD)** is defined as AD with symptom onset **before age 65** and accounts for ~5-10% of all AD cases. When there is a strong autosomal dominant family history (multiple affected members across generations with onset <65), genetic testing should be pursued after appropriate **genetic counseling**. The most common mutations are: (1) **PSEN1** (presenilin-1, chromosome 14) — accounts for ~80% of early-onset familial AD cases, with nearly 100% penetrance; (2) **APP** (chromosome 21) — ~10-15%; (3) **PSEN2** (chromosome 1) — rarest, variable penetrance. APOE ε4 is a **risk factor** for late-onset sporadic AD but is NOT deterministic and NOT routinely used for diagnosis. C9orf72 is associated with FTD and ALS, not AD. Posterior cortical atrophy (PCA) is a recognized variant of early-onset AD presenting with prominent visuospatial deficits. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old man with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, atrial fibrillation, and a prior stroke presents with a **stepwise decline** in cognitive function over the past 3 years. The family reports episodes of sudden worsening followed by plateaus. Examination reveals left-sided upper extremity weakness and hyperreflexia. Brain MRI shows **bilateral basal ganglia lacunes and confluent periventricular white matter disease**. Which of the following is the primary management strategy?', '["Immediate ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement to relieve cerebral edema from chronic ischemia", "Aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification including blood pressure control (<130/80 mmHg), anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation, high-intensity statin therapy, and glycemic optimization to prevent further cerebrovascular events", "High-dose cholinesterase inhibitor monotherapy as first-line curative treatment for vascular dementia", "Initiation of high-dose intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) to reduce cerebral vasculitis", "Empiric levodopa-carbidopa therapy to address the executive dysfunction and gait abnormalities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has **vascular dementia (VaD)**, the second most common cause of dementia after Alzheimer''''s disease. The hallmarks include: (1) **Stepwise cognitive decline** with sudden worsenings temporally related to cerebrovascular events followed by plateaus, (2) **Focal neurological signs** on examination, and (3) **Cerebrovascular disease on neuroimaging** (multiple infarcts, white matter hyperintensities). The **primary management** is **secondary stroke prevention** through aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification: BP control (<130/80 per AHA/ASA), anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation (e.g., apixaban), high-intensity statins, glycemic control (HbA1c <7%), smoking cessation, and antiplatelet therapy if not anticoagulated. Cholinesterase inhibitors have shown only modest and inconsistent benefits in VaD and are NOT first-line. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old woman with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, atrial fibrillation, and a prior stroke presents with a **stepwise decline** in cognitive function over the past 3 years. The family reports episodes of sudden worsening followed by plateaus. Examination reveals left-sided upper extremity weakness and hyperreflexia. Brain MRI shows **multiple lacunar infarcts and diffuse white matter hyperintensities**. Which of the following is the primary management strategy?', '["Initiation of high-dose intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) to reduce cerebral vasculitis", "Immediate ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement to relieve cerebral edema from chronic ischemia", "Empiric levodopa-carbidopa therapy to address the executive dysfunction and gait abnormalities", "Aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification including blood pressure control (<130/80 mmHg), anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation, high-intensity statin therapy, and glycemic optimization to prevent further cerebrovascular events", "High-dose cholinesterase inhibitor monotherapy as first-line curative treatment for vascular dementia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has **vascular dementia (VaD)**, the second most common cause of dementia after Alzheimer''''s disease. The hallmarks include: (1) **Stepwise cognitive decline** with sudden worsenings temporally related to cerebrovascular events followed by plateaus, (2) **Focal neurological signs** on examination, and (3) **Cerebrovascular disease on neuroimaging** (multiple infarcts, white matter hyperintensities). The **primary management** is **secondary stroke prevention** through aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification: BP control (<130/80 per AHA/ASA), anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation (e.g., apixaban), high-intensity statins, glycemic control (HbA1c <7%), smoking cessation, and antiplatelet therapy if not anticoagulated. Cholinesterase inhibitors have shown only modest and inconsistent benefits in VaD and are NOT first-line. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
