e NOT first-line. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old man with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, atrial fibrillation, and a prior stroke presents with a **stepwise decline** in cognitive function over the past 2 years. The family reports episodes of sudden worsening followed by plateaus. Examination reveals right visual field cut (homonymous hemianopia) and mild dysarthria. Brain MRI shows **periventricular ischemic changes and old subcortical infarcts**. Which of the following is the primary management strategy?', '["Empiric levodopa-carbidopa therapy to address the executive dysfunction and gait abnormalities", "High-dose cholinesterase inhibitor monotherapy as first-line curative treatment for vascular dementia", "Immediate ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement to relieve cerebral edema from chronic ischemia", "Aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification including blood pressure control (<130/80 mmHg), anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation, high-intensity statin therapy, and glycemic optimization to prevent further cerebrovascular events", "Initiation of high-dose intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) to reduce cerebral vasculitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has **vascular dementia (VaD)**, the second most common cause of dementia after Alzheimer''''s disease. The hallmarks include: (1) **Stepwise cognitive decline** with sudden worsenings temporally related to cerebrovascular events followed by plateaus, (2) **Focal neurological signs** on examination, and (3) **Cerebrovascular disease on neuroimaging** (multiple infarcts, white matter hyperintensities). The **primary management** is **secondary stroke prevention** through aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification: BP control (<130/80 per AHA/ASA), anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation (e.g., apixaban), high-intensity statins, glycemic control (HbA1c <7%), smoking cessation, and antiplatelet therapy if not anticoagulated. Cholinesterase inhibitors have shown only modest and inconsistent benefits in VaD and are NOT first-line. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old male with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, atrial fibrillation, and a prior stroke presents with a **stepwise decline** in cognitive function over the past 4 years. The family reports episodes of sudden worsening followed by plateaus. Examination reveals left-sided facial droop and pronator drift. Brain MRI shows **periventricular ischemic changes and old subcortical infarcts**. Which of the following is the primary management strategy?', '["Aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification including blood pressure control (<130/80 mmHg), anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation, high-intensity statin therapy, and glycemic optimization to prevent further cerebrovascular events", "Immediate ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement to relieve cerebral edema from chronic ischemia", "Empiric levodopa-carbidopa therapy to address the executive dysfunction and gait abnormalities", "High-dose cholinesterase inhibitor monotherapy as first-line curative treatment for vascular dementia", "Initiation of high-dose intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) to reduce cerebral vasculitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has **vascular dementia (VaD)**, the second most common cause of dementia after Alzheimer''''s disease. The hallmarks include: (1) **Stepwise cognitive decline** with sudden worsenings temporally related to cerebrovascular events followed by plateaus, (2) **Focal neurological signs** on examination, and (3) **Cerebrovascular disease on neuroimaging** (multiple infarcts, white matter hyperintensities). The **primary management** is **secondary stroke prevention** through aggressive cardiovascular risk factor modification: BP control (<130/80 per AHA/ASA), anticoagulation for atrial fibrillation (e.g., apixaban), high-intensity statins, glycemic control (HbA1c <7%), smoking cessation, and antiplatelet therapy if not anticoagulated. Cholinesterase inhibitors have shown only modest and inconsistent benefits in VaD and are NOT first-line. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Master the Boards Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male with a history of hypertension develops an **acute onset** of confusion, memory impairment, and apathy following a witnessed neurological event. CT angiography confirms an acute ischemic stroke. Brain MRI shows a single infarct in the **left thalamus (anterior and dorsomedial nuclei)**. Cognitive testing 3 months later reveals persistent executive dysfunction and memory impairment. Which of the following best explains this presentation?', '["Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease \u2014 the acute presentation and rapid cognitive decline indicate prion disease triggered by cerebral ischemia", "Multi-infarct dementia \u2014 requires at least five separate strokes in different vascular territories before cognitive impairment occurs", "Strategic single-infarct dementia \u2014 a single stroke in a cognitively critical brain region (such as the thalamus, angular gyrus, or hippocampus) can cause significant cognitive impairment disproportionate to lesion size", "Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 the stroke was coincidental and the cognitive decline is due to underlying neurodegenerative amyloid pathology unrelated to the infarct", "Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus \u2014 the single infarct disrupted CSF flow pathways, causing acute communicating hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Strategic single-infarct dementia** is a recognized subtype of vascular cognitive impairment in which a **single stroke** in a **cognitively critical (''''strategic'''') brain region** produces significant cognitive impairment disproportionate to the small lesion size. Key strategic locations include: (1) **Bilateral thalamus** (especially the anterior and dorsomedial nuclei) — memory, executive function, and arousal; (2) **Angular gyrus** (dominant hemisphere) — Gerstmann syndrome (agraphia, acalculia, left-right confusion, finger agnosia) plus alexia and semantic processing deficits; (3) **Hippocampus** — acute amnesia; (4) **Caudate nucleus** — executive dysfunction, apathy, and behavioral changes; (5) **Anterior cerebral artery territory** — abulia, executive dysfunction. Management focuses on secondary stroke prevention and cognitive rehabilitation. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female with a history of hypertension develops an **acute onset** of confusion, memory impairment, and apathy following a witnessed neurological event. CT angiography confirms an acute ischemic stroke. Brain MRI shows a single infarct in the **bilateral hippocampi**. Cognitive testing 3 months later reveals persistent executive dysfunction and memory impairment. Which of the following best explains this presentation?', '["Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 the stroke was coincidental and the cognitive decline is due to underlying neurodegenerative amyloid pathology unrelated to the infarct", "Strategic single-infarct dementia \u2014 a single stroke in a cognitively critical brain region (such as the thalamus, angular gyrus, or hippocampus) can cause significant cognitive impairment disproportionate to lesion size", "Multi-infarct dementia \u2014 requires at least five separate strokes in different vascular territories before cognitive impairment occurs", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease \u2014 the acute presentation and rapid cognitive decline indicate prion disease triggered by cerebral ischemia", "Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus \u2014 the single infarct disrupted CSF flow pathways, causing acute communicating hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Strategic single-infarct dementia** is a recognized subtype of vascular cognitive impairment in which a **single stroke** in a **cognitively critical (''''strategic'''') brain region** produces significant cognitive impairment disproportionate to the small lesion size. Key strategic locations include: (1) **Bilateral thalamus** (especially the anterior and dorsomedial nuclei) — memory, executive function, and arousal; (2) **Angular gyrus** (dominant hemisphere) — Gerstmann syndrome (agraphia, acalculia, left-right confusion, finger agnosia) plus alexia and semantic processing deficits; (3) **Hippocampus** — acute amnesia; (4) **Caudate nucleus** — executive dysfunction, apathy, and behavioral changes; (5) **Anterior cerebral artery territory** — abulia, executive dysfunction. Management focuses on secondary stroke prevention and cognitive rehabilitation. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
